Readable file still denied|The service belongs to the file's group but cannot open a mode-640 configuration.|namei -l /srv/app/config.yml|Compare the service UID/groups with each path component. A parent directory without search permission blocks access before the file's read bit matters. Also inspect ACL effective rights when present.|Grant the intended identity narrowly scoped traversal and file access; preserve restrictions for other users. Do not apply recursive 777.|Open the file as the actual service identity and confirm unrelated identities remain denied.
Disk full after deleting logs|df reports nearly full storage while du shows much less visible data.|lsof +L1|Find large deleted files still open by a process and confirm their device matches the full mount. Also account for snapshots and mount boundaries.|Use the application's supported log reopen or a controlled restart after assessing impact. Correct rotation so future logs reopen properly.|Confirm free blocks recover and new logs continue arriving at the expected path.
No space with free gigabytes|Creating files fails even though byte capacity remains.|df -i|Check IFree and IUse% on the affected mount. Identify a directory generating excessive small files before deleting anything.|Remove only confirmed disposable files under a retention policy and fix the producer or lifecycle rule. Plan filesystem capacity if the workload is legitimate.|Confirm inode headroom and successful file creation; observe growth over the next workload cycle.
High load and idle CPU|Requests slow down while load average rises but CPU remains mostly idle.|vmstat 1 5|Inspect blocked tasks, I/O waits and storage latency. Correlate D-state processes with a device or network filesystem; load alone does not identify the bottleneck.|Reduce or pause the offending I/O workload, restore the storage path, or shift traffic using a tested procedure. Avoid treating CPU expansion as the first fix.|Verify blocked-task count, storage latency and application response times recover together.
Too many open files|A service rejects connections with descriptor-allocation errors.|cat /proc/PID/limits|Compare the actual process limit with descriptor counts and types over time. A steadily rising count under stable traffic suggests a leak; a bounded peak may be legitimate demand.|Fix leaked handles or unbounded connection creation. If demand is valid, raise the service limit within host capacity and roll out safely.|Repeat the workload and verify descriptor counts stabilize below the limit without increased host pressure.
Exit 137 after deployment|A containerized process disappears with exit status 137.|journalctl -k --since '-15 min'|Look for a matching OOM event and inspect the relevant cgroup memory.events and limit. Also check operator or orchestrator termination; the exit code alone only suggests SIGKILL.|If memory pressure is confirmed, roll back a regression or adjust measured capacity and concurrency. If another actor killed it, correct that lifecycle behavior.|Verify the termination reason no longer recurs and memory/latency remain stable at representative load.
Local service unreachable remotely|curl on localhost succeeds but clients on another host fail.|ss -lntp|Check whether the listener binds only to 127.0.0.1 and confirm the intended port. If it listens externally, inspect routing and firewall evidence next.|Bind to the intended interface and apply only the required network access rules. Do not disable the firewall globally.|Test from an authorized remote client and verify unauthorized networks remain blocked.
Read-only filesystem|Writes fail with read-only filesystem after device warnings.|findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS|Confirm the mount is ro and correlate kernel messages with the backing device. Distinguish an intentional read-only mount from protective remount after errors.|Protect recoverable data, stop affected writers and follow filesystem-specific recovery. Do not force a writable remount while underlying corruption remains unresolved.|Validate filesystem health and restored writes through an application-level check before returning full traffic.
Zombie accumulation|A service accumulates many Z-state child processes.|ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm|Group zombies by parent PID and check whether the parent collects child exit status. The children have already exited and cannot be repaired by another kill signal.|Correct the parent's wait/reaping behavior; use a controlled parent restart as mitigation if appropriate. Container init behavior may need correction.|Run repeated child workloads and confirm process-table entries are reaped rather than accumulating.
Latency under CPU quota|A service's latency rises even though host cores are idle.|cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat|Read the affected cgroup's throttling counters over time, not necessarily the root cgroup. Compare quota, concurrency and latency in the same interval.|Tune quota or workload concurrency based on demand and host capacity. Preserve isolation requirements rather than removing all limits.|Verify throttling and tail latency improve under representative traffic without starving neighbors.
